t:"The Romans named each day by counting down to the next Kalends (the 1st), Nones (the 5th, or 7th in March, May, July and October) or Ides (the 13th or 15th), counting both ends: so 16 October is \u201Ca.d. XVII Kal. Nov.\u201D, the seventeenth day before the Kalends of November. Here that reckoning is applied to today\u2019s ordinary date, as modern Roman polytheists do; the Julian calendar the Romans used now runs 13 days behind ours, so these are not the dates an ancient Roman would have seen on the same day. The festivals are those of the inscribed calendars, Ovid and Varro, kept on their traditional dates. In a leap year February still has 28 named days: the sixth day before the March Kalends is said twice, and the added one, \u201Ca.d. bis VI Kal. Mart.\u201D (whence \u201Cbissextile\u201D), is given here as 24 February, after the Terminalia, as Censorinus has it; the jurist Celsus can be read as putting it a day later. February festivals counted from the Kalends after it (the Regifugium, the Equirria) then fall a day later. A few festivals were movable (the Compitalia given here on its later fixed days; the Feriae Latinae, the Fornacalia and the Sementivae, set each year, are left out) or changed length over the centuries, and the meaning of some old rites was already uncertain to the Romans themselves. The year is counted from the founding of Rome (AUC), as Romans later sometimes did; ordinarily they named the year by its consuls.",sources:["H. H. Scullard, Festivals and Ceremonies of the Roman Republic (1981)","Ovid, Fasti; Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.12\u201316; Varro, De Lingua Latina 6","A. K. Michels, The Calendar of the Roman Republic (1967)","https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Calendarium.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_festivals","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_calendar","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_calendar (the bissextile day: Celsus, Censorinus, De Die Natali 20)"],dateOf:e=>Ll(e),eventsBetw